From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 19 Aug 94 02:44:09 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-216] Does Existence Exist?

Another Golden-Oldie thread title that serves as a CyberMilepost, showing just
how tremendously far we have progressed from those silly old pre-Socratic
Greeks.  We must be through the Singularity and on the other side now.

Robin Hanson wrote 8/18/94: <Consider the set of all
possible/imaginable/self-consistent "universes", where a "universe" is a total
space-time-whatever state
description (including the physical laws applicable there).  Each of this kind
of "universe" may include many other of other kinds of "universes", such as
including many disconnected space-time regions, or a quantum superposition of
many worlds/histories.  A universe might be specified by a program and a
computer it runs on, for those who like to think in such terms.

A fundamental question one can ask is:  What things in what universes
subjectively experience what?>

A term I have seen used by researchers doing this kind of modelling, is
"artificial realities."  The limited attempts to answer the fundamental question
posed above, that I have seen so far, have been within a context of functionally
integrated interactions by self-contained entities with their environment and
other self-contained entities.  If the self-contained entities are programmable,
I would question whether they are capable of subjective experience because they
are not unique, they are functionally deterministic.  But, I have never seen a
good definition of "programmable" or "program" so I don't know for certain what
I am saying here.  I would like to see a good definition someday, so that I
might begin to figure out what I want to think about this important topic of
subjective artificial realities.  My guess now, in the absence of good
definitions, is that all experience in artificial realities is objective, and
that only within real existence (i.e., not simulated, modelled or hypothesized)
do we find subjectivity.  I am certain that I am uncertain about this.

