From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 1994 03:11:57 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-8-217] Smart Contracts

Vince Kerchner adds several great ideas to our growing smart 
contract design toolbox.  At this point we should distinguish between 
the online and offline parts of smart contracts.  The online segment
is the transaction protocol that implements or enforces terms
of the contract.  For example, the digital cash protocol can
be the payment element of the online segment.  The offline
segment can include contract drafting tools such as Vince has 
proposed: legal databases, templates, customization, and verification 
of legality in various jurisdictions; credit/reputation checks; etc.  

Synthetic assets (which I described in the esssay) are made possible 
by offline analysis of complicated payment schedules. They also have 
an online segment, typically consisting of DES-encrypted messages
beamed between banks, brokers, and markets in various EDI message formats.
For synthetic assets, it's the offline analysis that makes the crucial 
difference, although it wasn't until recently that we could take
for granted cheap, secure, and nearly instant communications between 
major brokerages and hedge funds.

Vince suggests implementing milestones, account info, and payment 
schedules for consulting contracts.  These contractual elements
can be analyzed offline, but they might also be brought to life 
in the online segment, along with transaction protocols built 
from cryptographic building blocks.

> 6.  To encode and transfer partial product "unlocking" passwords.  In
> exchange for payment, contractees could automatically transfer to employers
> a series of passwords or keys which enable or decode the work for each
> milestone.  Currently, this would only be possible for work consisting of
> software, firmware, or pure information.

This is a nice idea for an online segment, which fits in nicely 
with the PERT/milestone model.  We might implement this on top 
of a source code control/versioning system (for example the Revision 
Control System (RCS) on Berkely Unix).  Note that even though RCS 
is primarily used for software development, the same general idea 
and even RCS itself can be used in other kinds of design and writing 
tasks.  RCS is currently designed for development
within an organization, but through smart contracts RCS might
used to coordinate the work of several independent consultants.

In general, most kinds of "groupware" such as RCS, multi-user
relational databases, workflow, etc. are designed for use within 
a single organization, usually within a hierarchical trust structure.  
It will be very interesting, and perhaps quite lucrative, to 
"externalize" these into smart contracts that facilitate work
shared between otherwise untrusted and competing organizations 
or individuals.  ("Externalize" here is a new coinage, not 
to be confused with the term of art from economics -- suggestions 
for a better word are solicited).

> 3.  To encode PPLs, personal violation penalties, and escrows. 

With crypto protocols, we may be able to replace trusted third 
party escrows in many cases.   This might be done with variations 
of the "Digital Certified Mail" and "Secret Sharing" protocols in 
Bruce Schneier's _Applied Cryptography_.  (I'd describe them here 
but they are quite involved, worth at least an essay apiece, and I'm 
still learning them myself.  Also, they are based on several of
the building blocks I listed earlier; learning those 
building blocks is a prerequisite for learning these protocols).

The design of new social institutions via smart contracts is 
very exciting.  To put it more accurately, we would be designing 
what F.A. Hayek calls a "catallaxy", or a group of strangers that form a 
limited community via a narrow communications channel (the contract).  
The catallaxy that emerges depends on the incentives provided 
by that contract.  The details of what will emerge are sometimes
hard to predict, but we can use contract theory, and experience from
historical legal, business, political, etc. structures, to 
help make such predictions, and guide our designs accordingly.

The most often cited examples of such channels are market prices, 
but more generally they can be any sort of signal sent by, or 
relationship conducted via, a contract. 

By reducing the need for trusted third parties, reducing
fraud, reducing vulnerability to non-party snooping or
attack, and other reductions of transaction costs, 
smart contracts will allow us to maintain a wider variety
of relationships with many more people than current market
and other contractual mechanisms.  In particular the
prospect of establishing worldwide private markets and PPLs
in cyberspace, for example Tim May's "Blacknet", is quite
exciting.

Two general categories of smart contracts are already booming:

* Arbitrage between utility functions.  Everybody has 
their own unique utility function, which (ideally) describes
what they want and when they want it.  Perhaps the fastest growing 
industry in the world is that of fixed income derivatives 
and synthetic assets.  The main function of these contracts is to 
arbitrage between people who want money now and those who can 
wait until later.  People often plan their own complicated personal
budgets (reflecting their own utility functions) on spreadsheets.
These are often implemented via credit cards, a flexible but costly 
and panoptic credit instrument.  I suspect that first business credit, 
and then personal credit will merge into the world of synthetic 
assets.  We may see the day when, via personal computer 
software and bidding agents, individuals design and trade their own 
personal synthetic asset portfolio.

* Arbitrage between jurisdictions.  This is currently done
with currency exchange and capital flows, another booming
market.  I foresee the day when personal budget software 
keeps score in multiple currencies, tied to bidding agents 
that shop around for the best currency deals, depending on 
the need (travel money, stable savings vehicle, tax savings, 
etc.)  Also, one can sometimes construct synthetic assets
that mimic assets available in a jurisdiction without being
exposed to costs imposed by that jurisdiction.  

