From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 1994 08:42:25 GMT
Subject: [#94-8-218] Does existence exist? 

Robin Hanson:
> For example, imagine we have a program on some computer that manages
> to have essentially the same input/output as an ordinary human brain,
> responding to the same space-time pattern of sensory input signals
> with the same space-time pattern of effector output signals.  If this
> program and computer is very analogous to biological human brains,
> we will likely believe this brain experiences the same sort of things
> we do.  
> 
> But I think many people are rather reluctant to believe in similar
> experiences, even when the input/output patterns are preserved, if the
> implementation of that pattern is different enough.

I think the existence [*] of some implementation is sufficient to ensure
that the experiences exist and are and similar, no matter how different
the implementation is.

> For example, imagine "playing back" the result of a previous
> simulation run.  Would this result in a new experience with the same
> content, or would it be the same identical experience?

The same identical experience, IMO.  The "experience" itself can't tell
if it is "new" or identical ("similar")  with the original.  These
alternatives are operationally indistinguishable by any conceivable
test, in principle and in practice, and therefore, perhaps, saying the
same thing.  I would not care if "I" had been emulated back in the
Devonian Era and my responses today generated by the ancient look-up
table.  I would have no way of detecting this.

> Imagine having run bizillions of previous simulations, and 
> interpolating from lookup table caches from those runs whenever
> the current situation is similar enough.  When would such a 
> program have experiences?  Granted these tables might be huge,
> but that's not the issue here.

I think the answer hinges on the use of "when" (assuming you are using
the word in the temporal sense, which you may not be), which is not
something detectable by the experiences themselves.  The *existence*
somewhere/when of the look-up tables implies that the mental states have
been emulated at some time, somewhere, in some sense.  Ergo the
experiences have been realised [*] and hence experienced.

> What if an improbable thermal fluctuation created the same pattern,
> now across just space instead of space-time, but without the same
> causal structure?  Would that pattern experience?

No, because there was/is/will be no implementation.

> And what transformations preserve the "same" pattern.  Substituting
> 1 for 0 and vice versa in I/O bit strings might be considered the
> same.  But if we allow any transformation, then any physical phenomena
> might be considered the "same" pattern.  

If a transformation transformed a sequences of 000000s into the required
I/O then I would conclude that the transformation encoded the
experiences.  So the important thing would be - although there might be
no way of establishing this - was/is the transform realised? 

[*] I haven't defined "exist" and "realised", because I don't know what
it means, so I haven't answered the original question posed.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

