From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 1994 10:29:59 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-220] Does existence exist? 

Michael Clive Price writes:
>> Imagine having run bizillions of previous simulations, and 
>> interpolating from lookup table caches from those runs whenever
>> the current situation is similar enough.  When would such a 
>> program have experiences?  
>
>I think the answer hinges on the use of "when" (assuming you are using
>the word in the temporal sense, which you may not be), which is not
>something detectable by the experiences themselves.  The *existence*
>somewhere/when of the look-up tables implies that the mental states have
>been emulated at some time, somewhere, in some sense.  Ergo the
>experiences have been realised [*] and hence experienced.

But the novel feature of this scenario is the use of *interpolation*.
None of the interpolated situations happened before during the prior
simulations, though *very* similar situations did.  Are there new
interpolated experiences cooresponding to the interpolated I/O
patterns generated?

>> What if an improbable thermal fluctuation created the same pattern,
>> now across just space instead of space-time, but without the same
>> causal structure?  Would that pattern experience?
>
>No, because there was/is/will be no implementation.

You must mean something special by "implementation".  In a literal
sense, the pattern happened, and therefore it had some implementation.

>> And what transformations preserve the "same" pattern.  Substituting
>> 1 for 0 and vice versa in I/O bit strings might be considered the
>> same.  But if we allow any transformation, then any physical phenomena
>> might be considered the "same" pattern.  
>
>If a transformation transformed a sequences of 000000s into the required
>I/O then I would conclude that the transformation encoded the
>experiences.  So the important thing would be - although there might be
>no way of establishing this - was/is the transform realised? 

But if I am willing to grant that some reference I/O pattern would have
experiences, I'll also grant that the bit-inverse of it also would,
even if there were no actual physical process doing the bit flipping.
If you always require the transform be "realized", then there is some
physical point where the reference I/0 pattern is physically there.

Reilly Jones writes:
>If the self-contained entities are programmable, I would question
>whether they are capable of subjective experience because they are not
>unique, they are functionally deterministic.  But, I have never seen a
>good definition of "programmable" or "program" so I don't know for
>certain what I am saying here.

One can reasonably think of the initial state of our universe as a
program, and can think of the physical laws of state evolution as the
computer.  Thus we *are* part of a program running on a computer.

Steve Witham writes:
>I think Minsky's point was just that whether a universe "exists" is
>irrelevant to questions about what it's like inside it.  

But there is the common, and it seems to me self-consistent, view that
it just isn't like anything inside inside a universe that doesn't exist.

>So Minsky drops a lot of baggage at the door, but you go in.

Huh?

Robin Hanson

